TITLE='Authentic title "Who Can Afford Rent In 2026? (The Math Nobody Wants To Admit)" is a dated affordability question with a hidden-truth parenthetical, a recurring channel formula. The script builds a well-sourced case against the 30% rule with composite budgets and housing-market data. TYPICAL (1.07x) against peers including "Your Coworkers Afford Their Lifestyle" (21K) and "Turn $10,000 Into $500,000" (16K). Association only; no CTR evidence.'
THUMB='Registered maxresdefault 1280x720 (HTTP 200, real image). White field. Left: the recurring cartoon man in a white shirt, eyes wide, mouth open. Right: a black headline "$3,000 RENT... WHO?" above a cash pile crossed with a red X and a small house with a "RENT" sign and question marks. The $3,000 figure is not used in the script (typical rent $1,910; Denver two-bedroom $2,050), so the thumbnail inflates the headline number. At 320x180 the headline and icons remain legible.'
SCRIPT='A rent-affordability explainer: renewal-letter hook, history of the 30% rule, Zillow typical rent vs renter incomes, JCHS cost burden, composite budgets (Kevin in Columbus, a Denver couple), RealPage algorithmic pricing and DOJ settlement, junk fees and the FTC rule, evictions, NLIHC housing wage, rent vs buy and mortgage amortisation, living with parents, seniors (Linda) and homelessness, the 2026 supply-driven softening and future tightening, and three actions. Verified: Zillow $1,910 and 1.8% growth, JCHS 22.6M/12.1M, RealPage settlement with no penalty. Reproduces: 30% income need, 45% burden, Kevin budget, eviction rate, first-year interest share. Problems: thumbnail $3,000 not in script; Denver -$938 not reproducible; FTC rule scope overstated; "five years ago essentially zero" down-payment claim; several statistics unfetched. The stored ending is complete.'
